WEBVTT

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:03.110
Preparation begins with cryptographic inventory.

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:07.240
An inventory records where cryptography is used, including algorithms, protocols,

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:11.655
libraries, certificates, keys, systems, services, devices, and dependencies.

00:00:11.655 --> 00:00:16.660
It should cover external-facing systems, internal service communication, VPNs, SSH,

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:21.220
code signing, firmware signing, identity platforms, cloud services, build pipelines,

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:23.830
embedded devices, and data protection workflows.

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:27.505
Cryptographic agility is the ability to change algorithms, keys, libraries,

00:00:27.505 --> 00:00:30.190
and protocols without redesigning the entire system.

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:33.240
Agility is partly technical, but it is also operational.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:37.740
It requires ownership, documentation, testing, configuration control, vendor engagement,

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:39.060
and deployment discipline.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.715
A system that can only use one algorithm forever is fragile,

00:00:41.715 --> 00:00:43.800
even if that algorithm is strong today.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.750
Post-quantum cryptography provides new algorithms intended to resist known quantum

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:48.430
attacks.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:49.560
During migration,

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:53.340
some environments may use hybrid approaches that combine classical and post-quantum

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:56.006
mechanisms to manage compatibility and transition risk.

00:00:56.006 --> 00:00:58.120
Hybrid designs still need careful testing.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:02.700
They can affect handshake sizes, certificate sizes, key management, performance, logging,

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:05.673
monitoring, and interoperability with older clients or devices.

00:01:05.673 --> 00:01:07.640
Vendor questions should be concrete.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:09.645
Which algorithms and standards are supported?

00:01:09.645 --> 00:01:10.960
Which products are in scope?

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:12.075
What timelines apply?

00:01:12.075 --> 00:01:13.685
Will updates be validated?

00:01:13.685 --> 00:01:15.830
How will certificates, keys, and APIs change?

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:17.680
What testing guidance is available?

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:20.890
A migration roadmap should connect those answers to pilot deployments,

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:24.730
compatibility testing, procurement requirements, change windows, rollback plans,

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:26.400
and long-term crypto agility.
